description:
From: Elixir Parity. User-approved 2026-11-25. Blocks fz-mue.67, and through it fz-nhb.15 (defguard) and #791.

Evidence (reproduced on develop a6f4c6edd):
  def f(args), do: quote(do: foo(unquote_splicing(args)))   ; f([1,2])
  def h(args), do: quote(do: [0, unquote_splicing(args)])   ; h([1,2])
Elixir 1.19.4 gives {:foo, [], [1, 2]} and [0, 1, 2]. fz gives {:foo, [], [{:unquote_splicing, [], [{:args, [], Main}]}]}, leaving the call in the AST, and warns that args is unused. In a def head (def unquote(name)(unquote_splicing(args))) it fails as internal/post-resolution-leftover. Kernel.define_guard uses exactly that head shape.

Done when: splices work everywhere Elixir allows them: call arguments, list elements (including before a | tail), tuple elements, block bodies and def heads. An oracle fixture matches Elixir on interp, run and build. Built first in the fz-mue.67 worktree (../fz-mue67).
